"Validation done. I read `model_v2.md` line by line and ran structural and dimensional checks. **Verdict: the model passes — no errors found, so no `model_v3.md` is needed.**\n\n## What I checked\n\n**1. Structural completeness** ✅\n- Variables, objective, explicit constraints, nonnegativity, compact form, and matrix form are all present.\n- §3 (detailed), §4 (compact), and §5 (matrix form) agree with each other: A = [[2,1],[1,3]] and b = [100, 90] reproduce exactly the two constraints in §3.1–3.2; c = [40, 50] matches the objective.\n\n**2. Dimensional consistency** ✅\n\n| Equation | LHS analysis | RHS | Consistent? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Z = 40·xA + 50·xB | USD/unit × units/week = USD/week | Z in USD/week | ✔ |\n| 2·xA + 1·xB ≤ 100 | h/unit × units/week = h/week | 100 h/week | ✔ |\n| 1·xA + 3·xB ≤ 90 | h/unit × units/week = h/week | 90 h/week | ✔ |\n| xA, xB ≥ 0 | units/week vs. dimensionless 0 | — | ✔ (zero is dimension-compatible) |\n\n**3. Coefficient transcription vs. v1 data** ✅\nMachine row (A: 2, B: 1 vs. 100 h), labor row (A: 1, B: 3 vs. 90 h), and profits (40, 50) all match `model_v1.md` exactly — no swapped or dropped coefficients. Within each equation, both terms share the same units, so no dimension mixing.\n\n**4. Matrix form** ✅\ncᵀx → USD/week; A·x → h/week; b in h/week. Dimensions chain correctly through the standard form.\n\n## Two minor observations (not errors)\n\n1. **b mixes resource types:** b = [100, 90]ᵀ combines machine-hours/week and labor-hours/week. Both are dimensionally *hours/week*, so this is consistent — but for extra clarity you could annotate per-entry: b = [100 machine-h/wk, 90 labor-h/wk]ᵀ. Standard LP practice; cosmetic only.\n2. **Nonnegativity vs. scalar zero:** comparing units/week to \"0\" is fine (0 admits any dimension), just worth knowing it's a convention, not an oversight.\n\n## Why no v3\n\nSince both findings are cosmetic annotations rather than structural or dimensional defects, creating `model_v3.md` would just add version churn with zero substantive change. If the model later evolves (e.g. adding demand limits or integrality), I'd fold those two annotation refinements into that next version.\n\nWant me to save this validation report as a file (e.g. `validation_v2.md`) for the audit trail, or move on to solving the model? 🦀"